WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.566
Building Confidence.

2
00:00:01.846 --> 00:00:06.219
Day 19 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Communication phase.

3
00:00:07.969 --> 00:00:08.993
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.273 --> 00:00:11.321
Confidence doesn't come before action.

5
00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:13.372
It comes from repeating the action.

6
00:00:13.652 --> 00:00:16.169
You don't need to feel confident to act confident.

7
00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.305
The feeling follows the practice.

8
00:00:19.655 --> 00:00:20.509
Part 1.

9
00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:23.157
Why you feel nervous (and why it's normal).

10
00:00:24.407 --> 00:00:25.580
Fear of judgement:

11
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.289
They'll think I don't know enough.

12
00:00:27.569 --> 00:00:30.641
Most people are thinking about themselves, not about you.

13
00:00:30.921 --> 00:00:35.465
Language anxiety: speaking in a second language makes everyone feel slower.

14
00:00:35.745 --> 00:00:38.817
Clear, simple English beats fancy English every time.

15
00:00:39.097 --> 00:00:42.745
Impostor syndrome: common among experienced developers too.

16
00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:48.593
A 15-year engineer may know the code well and still feel lost in a room of executives.

17
00:00:48.873 --> 00:00:53.673
Lack of preparation: this is the most common cause, and the easiest one to fix.

18
00:00:55.023 --> 00:00:55.898
Part 2.

19
00:00:56.178 --> 00:00:57.373
The confidence loop.

20
00:00:58.623 --> 00:00:59.540
Step 1:

21
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.809
Prepare: write 3 bullet points before any important conversation.

22
00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:05.007
Step 2:

23
00:01:05.287 --> 00:01:07.356
Act: speak up once, even briefly.

24
00:01:07.636 --> 00:01:08.553
Step 3:

25
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:11.457
Survive: notice that nothing terrible happened.

26
00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:12.697
Step 4:

27
00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:15.964
Reflect: one thing that went well, one to improve.

28
00:01:16.244 --> 00:01:17.204
Step 5:

29
00:01:17.484 --> 00:01:19.980
Repeat: each loop makes the next one easier.

30
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.226
Part 3.

31
00:01:22.506 --> 00:01:23.935
10 practical habits.

32
00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:27.596
Speak in the first 5 minutes of every meeting.

33
00:01:27.876 --> 00:01:31.759
Even Good morning, everyone or I agree with Rahul's point counts.

34
00:01:32.039 --> 00:01:34.172
The longer you wait, the harder it gets.

35
00:01:34.452 --> 00:01:39.572
Prepare 3 bullet points before any one-on-one, demo, or difficult conversation.

36
00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:41.601
Slow down by 20%.

37
00:01:41.881 --> 00:01:43.545
Nervous people speak fast.

38
00:01:43.825 --> 00:01:45.831
A slow pace sounds calm and senior.

39
00:01:46.111 --> 00:01:47.433
Pause instead of filling.

40
00:01:47.713 --> 00:01:49.591
Swap umm for a 1-second pause.

41
00:01:49.871 --> 00:01:51.236
Pauses sound thoughtful.

42
00:01:52.586 --> 00:01:54.250
Stop over-apologising.

43
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.343
Keep sorry for real mistakes.

44
00:01:56.623 --> 00:01:59.439
Swap Sorry for the delay for Thanks for your patience.

45
00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:00.850
Keep a wins file.

46
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:05.418
Write down every bug you fixed, compliment you got, problem you solved.

47
00:02:05.698 --> 00:02:07.831
Read it before reviews and interviews.

48
00:02:08.111 --> 00:02:10.287
Say I don't know, I'll find out by X.

49
00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:11.997
It's a confident sentence.

50
00:02:12.277 --> 00:02:15.093
Record yourself explaining a concept for 2 minutes.

51
00:02:15.373 --> 00:02:16.418
Watch it once.

52
00:02:16.698 --> 00:02:18.618
You'll fix 3 habits immediately.

53
00:02:18.898 --> 00:02:24.701
Volunteer for small visibility: a 5-minute demo, a stand-up summary, a lunch-and-learn.

54
00:02:26.051 --> 00:02:30.851
Talk to strangers in low-stakes places: a café, a train, an airport.

55
00:02:31.131 --> 00:02:33.307
It trains the same muscle you use at work.

56
00:02:33.587 --> 00:02:34.845
See Everyday Life.

57
00:02:36.195 --> 00:02:37.113
Part 4.

58
00:02:37.393 --> 00:02:38.886
Body language and voice.

59
00:02:40.136 --> 00:02:41.501
Here is a quick table:

60
00:02:41.781 --> 00:02:44.683
Signal, and Low confidence, and High confidence.

61
00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:45.859
Posture.

62
00:02:46.139 --> 00:02:47.269
Low confidence:

63
00:02:47.549 --> 00:02:48.957
Hunched, arms crossed.

64
00:02:49.237 --> 00:02:50.389
High confidence:

65
00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:53.101
Upright, shoulders relaxed, open hands.

66
00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:54.192
Eyes.

67
00:02:54.472 --> 00:02:55.603
Low confidence:

68
00:02:55.883 --> 00:02:57.504
Looking down or at the screen.

69
00:02:57.784 --> 00:02:58.936
High confidence:

70
00:02:59.216 --> 00:03:02.501
Natural eye contact (3 to 5 s, then look away).

71
00:03:02.781 --> 00:03:03.571
Voice.

72
00:03:03.851 --> 00:03:04.981
Low confidence:

73
00:03:05.261 --> 00:03:07.395
Rising tone at the end (It's done?).

74
00:03:07.675 --> 00:03:08.827
High confidence:

75
00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:11.197
Falling tone at the end (It's done.).

76
00:03:11.477 --> 00:03:12.267
Pace.

77
00:03:12.547 --> 00:03:13.677
Low confidence:

78
00:03:13.957 --> 00:03:15.259
Rushed, no pauses.

79
00:03:15.539 --> 00:03:16.691
High confidence:

80
00:03:16.971 --> 00:03:19.211
Steady, with pauses before key points.

81
00:03:19.491 --> 00:03:20.301
Hands.

82
00:03:20.581 --> 00:03:21.712
Low confidence:

83
00:03:21.992 --> 00:03:23.592
Fidgeting, touching face.

84
00:03:23.872 --> 00:03:25.024
High confidence:

85
00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:27.437
Still, or gesturing at waist level.

86
00:03:27.717 --> 00:03:28.720
Video calls.

87
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.131
Low confidence:

88
00:03:30.411 --> 00:03:32.544
Camera off, mic muted until called.

89
00:03:32.824 --> 00:03:33.976
High confidence:

90
00:03:34.256 --> 00:03:36.923
Camera on, eyes on lens when making a point.

91
00:03:38.273 --> 00:03:38.913
Tip:

92
00:03:39.193 --> 00:03:41.561
The 2-minute reset (before a big meeting).

93
00:03:41.841 --> 00:03:42.694
Stand up.

94
00:03:42.974 --> 00:03:44.318
Roll your shoulders back.

95
00:03:44.598 --> 00:03:48.331
Breathe in for 4, hold for 4, out for 6, three times.

96
00:03:48.611 --> 00:03:50.595
Say your first sentence out loud once.

97
00:03:50.875 --> 00:03:51.963
Remind yourself:

98
00:03:52.243 --> 00:03:54.547
I'm here because I know something useful.

99
00:03:55.897 --> 00:03:56.857
Part 5.

100
00:03:57.137 --> 00:03:59.143
Reframes for negative self-talk.

101
00:04:00.393 --> 00:04:01.758
Here is a quick table:

102
00:04:02.038 --> 00:04:03.275
Thought, and Reframe.

103
00:04:03.555 --> 00:04:05.070
My English isn't good enough.:

104
00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:06.801
Clear and simple is enough.

105
00:04:07.081 --> 00:04:08.638
People care about the idea.

106
00:04:08.918 --> 00:04:11.051
They'll find out I don't know everything.:

107
00:04:11.331 --> 00:04:12.803
Nobody knows everything.

108
00:04:13.083 --> 00:04:15.515
Asking good questions is a senior skill.

109
00:04:15.795 --> 00:04:17.481
I made a mistake in that meeting.:

110
00:04:17.761 --> 00:04:20.150
I noticed it, so I can fix it next time.

111
00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.390
That's growth.

112
00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.867
I'm too junior to speak to the CEO.:

113
00:04:24.147 --> 00:04:27.731
The CEO wants information from people closest to the work.

114
00:04:28.011 --> 00:04:28.865
That's me.

115
00:04:29.145 --> 00:04:32.195
I've been doing this 15 years, I should be better at this.:

116
00:04:32.475 --> 00:04:34.523
Communication is a separate skill.

117
00:04:34.803 --> 00:04:37.427
I can learn it just like I learned a new framework.

118
00:04:38.777 --> 00:04:39.716
Part 6.

119
00:04:39.996 --> 00:04:42.087
Confidence for experienced engineers.

120
00:04:43.337 --> 00:04:44.894
Seniors have a different trap.

121
00:04:45.174 --> 00:04:51.830
They know so much that they over-explain, get defensive about legacy decisions, or go quiet among executives.

122
00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:52.857
Try:

123
00:04:53.137 --> 00:04:54.459
Be brief on purpose.

124
00:04:54.739 --> 00:04:57.342
Your expertise shows in what you choose to leave out.

125
00:04:57.622 --> 00:04:59.030
Say I was wrong in public.

126
00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.382
Admitting a mistake makes you look more credible, not less.

127
00:05:02.662 --> 00:05:06.843
Sponsor, don't just mentor: speak juniors' names in rooms they're not in.

128
00:05:07.123 --> 00:05:10.515
Ask for feedback on your communication, not just your code.

129
00:05:11.865 --> 00:05:12.804
Part 7.

130
00:05:13.084 --> 00:05:14.471
Practice conversation:

131
00:05:14.751 --> 00:05:16.393
Speaking up early in a meeting.

132
00:05:17.643 --> 00:05:19.670
Here is how the conversation goes.

133
00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.017
Let's start.

134
00:05:21.297 --> 00:05:23.686
Any updates before we discuss the roadmap?

135
00:05:23.966 --> 00:05:25.587
Yes, one quick update.

136
00:05:25.867 --> 00:05:30.390
The search API is live in staging, and it's 40% faster.

137
00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:31.758
Great news.

138
00:05:32.038 --> 00:05:33.062
Any risks?

139
00:05:33.342 --> 00:05:34.046
One.

140
00:05:34.326 --> 00:05:39.873
The old mobile app version doesn't support it, so we'll need a fallback for about two weeks.

141
00:05:41.223 --> 00:05:42.823
The conversation continues.

142
00:05:43.103 --> 00:05:44.105
Good point.

143
00:05:44.385 --> 00:05:45.729
Can you write that up?

144
00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:48.313
Sure, I'll share it after this meeting.

145
00:05:49.663 --> 00:05:51.199
That's the end of this lesson.

146
00:05:51.479 --> 00:05:56.151
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

147
00:05:56.431 --> 00:06:02.533
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

